WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.890
- Could you tell if a person
is physically attractive?

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.600
- No. (laughs)

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.500
You don't go around being
like, "Nice to meet you."

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.480
Just had to clear that up. (giggles)

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.830
- If you were in a room alone

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.530
with someone who was
blind, what would you ask?

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.411
This video was sponsored by Google.

00:00:18.411 --> 00:00:21.480
(upbeat music)

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.100
Hey good humans, Milky here.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.870
As many of our Jubilee fans know,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.760
we prioritize inclusion

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.010
and creating empathy-driven conversations.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.360
That's why I'm excited to talk

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.800
about our new partnership with Google

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.170
in this special episode
of Ask Me Anything.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.430
In this collaboration,

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.050
Google and Jubilee are both committed

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.090
to creating a more accessible world.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.700
One of Jubilee's goals is
to be a space of belonging

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.320
for everyone through the power of deep

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.640
and meaningful dialogue.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.650
But we wanna make having these discussions

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.570
truly accessible for all.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.209
Accessibility has been
a priority for Google.

00:00:51.209 --> 00:00:53.970
It's a mindset and in
every evolving journey,

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.464
especially in eliminating stereotypes

00:00:55.464 --> 00:00:58.800
in the representation of
people with disabilities.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.600
And in this episode of Ask Me Anything,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.010
look out for moments where our hero

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.410
uses the Google accessibility features.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.030
For people in the disabled community

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.304
who need easy accessibility,

00:01:07.304 --> 00:01:10.200
the voice access is there
to help control your phone.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.730
Simply by talking out loud.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.231
Starting with...
- Hey, Google. Voice access.

00:01:14.231 --> 00:01:15.900
- Features like the voice guidance,

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.450
which empowers individuals
who are blind or low vision,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.250
to get around with detailed
voice announcements

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.110
for walking trips with Google Maps.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.780
And the talk-back feature,
which speaks aloud the screen,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.270
navigates through apps and
facilitates communication

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.100
with braille voice and keyboard.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.810
Google cares about what they make,

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.910
making sure it is for everyone.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.070
Because in a world where everyone belongs,

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.900
anything is possible.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.864
So to learn more about how
Google is creating a world

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:40.595
where everyone can belong,
check out the link,

00:01:40.595 --> 00:01:43.710
Belonging.Google in the description below

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.330
and then let's get into the video.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:46.200
- Hi.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.580
- My name is Mason.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:48.990
- My name's Precious. Nice to meet you.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.383
- Nice to meet you. How old are you?

00:01:50.383 --> 00:01:52.230
- I am 24.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.910
- Do you mind me asking you

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.100
what's your profession and
what you do for a living?

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.413
- So, I am a singer-songwriter

00:01:59.413 --> 00:02:01.950
and I also am a music educator.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:06.300
And I'm membership chair of
a coalition called RAMPD,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.500
which stands for Recording Artists

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.270
and Music Professionals with Disabilities.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:14.130
And my personal goal is to
be the first blind Latina

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.110
at the forefront of the
Latin music industry.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.444
- So have you always been blind?

00:02:18.444 --> 00:02:21.966
- Yeah, so I was born blind.

00:02:21.966 --> 00:02:24.000
I was actually born early.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.619
They had to give me
oxygen and as a result,

00:02:25.619 --> 00:02:27.150
my retinas kind of detached

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.590
and my eyes weren't fully developed.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.804
And as far as color, I
can't see any colors,

00:02:31.804 --> 00:02:35.728
but I kind of think of
colors by association.

00:02:35.728 --> 00:02:40.728
So the sky is blue, but then
like turquoise is the ocean.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.420
Pink is my favorite color.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.660
- I was gonna ask you, even
though you can't see colors,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.000
what color would you use
to describe yourself?

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.140
- Oh yeah, definitely pink.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.195
- Definitely pink.
- Definitely pink.

00:02:50.195 --> 00:02:51.750
(Precious giggling)
- Pink's a great color.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.070
It's my favorite color too.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:53.988
- Oh, cool.

00:02:53.988 --> 00:02:56.700
- Do you experience any visual sensations

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.223
when you play or listen to music?

00:02:58.223 --> 00:02:59.407
- I do not.

00:02:59.407 --> 00:03:01.866
- You don't?
- I don't have synesthesia,

00:03:01.866 --> 00:03:03.150
unfortunately.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:08.100
So synesthesia is when you
hear music or play music,

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:13.100
music has a taste or a visual or a color.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.160
Not something I have,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.530
but there are people that
do have it. (giggles)

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:20.520
- Are you still able to daydream
or see images in your head?

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.430
- In my dreams, I don't see anything.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.170
So, because I've never had sight,

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:29.113
everything that happens is
made up by all of the images

00:03:29.113 --> 00:03:31.260
that I've conjured in my head

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.450
based on soundscapes and what I've heard

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.610
and people's voices and
all these different things.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.490
- What's the most frustrating
about being blind?

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.223
- How everybody else looks at me.

00:03:40.223 --> 00:03:43.170
Like how people who don't
understand or don't know

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:47.366
or are ignorant about it
think of me as incapable

00:03:47.366 --> 00:03:49.470
or they view me as a child.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.940
Something called infantilization,

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.330
which views disabled people as children.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.460
- How much harder would you say it is

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.550
to be in the music industry
as a blind musician?

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:02.070
- We do have to work 10 times harder.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:03.967
It's not just thinking about,

00:04:03.967 --> 00:04:05.940
"Am I ready for this
gig? Or am I prepared?"

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.310
It's, "Can I get access to this gig?

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.470
Is there gonna be
somebody to help me around

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.420
if I don't know how to get around

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.100
'cause I'm not familiar with the gig?

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.260
Will I not get the gig
just because I'm disabled,

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.465
but nobody's actually gonna
tell me that's the reason?"

00:04:18.465 --> 00:04:20.610
There's a lot of different things

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.710
and as far as accessibility
within the industry,

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:25.380
it's not something that's universal yet.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.130
- So my next question is
how's the dating scene for you

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.420
as a blind individual?

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.590
- So for me, I'm actually
about to get married.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.481
So, I have a fiance.
- Congrats, congrats.

00:04:37.481 --> 00:04:40.230
- How did you meet your
husband or your fiance?

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:44.160
- We met through a mutual
friend like eight years ago.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.230
- That's awesome. And he's also blind?

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.160
- He is, yeah.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.690
It's really awesome

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.426
because you don't have to explain

00:04:50.426 --> 00:04:54.001
all the intricacies of how
something feels sometimes.

00:04:54.001 --> 00:04:56.147
'Cause if you're not going through it,

00:04:56.147 --> 00:04:59.630
some things people just can't
really understand fully.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:03.480
Sometimes people assume that
you have a sighted partner

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:04.904
that's gonna take care of you.

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:06.780
I was getting my nails done the other day

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:07.927
and the lady was like,

00:05:07.927 --> 00:05:09.896
"Your husband's gonna be
happy to see your nails."

00:05:09.896 --> 00:05:14.160
And I was like, (giggles)
"You don't know." (giggles)

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.782
- Could you tell if a person
is physically attractive?

00:05:18.782 --> 00:05:21.090
- No. (giggles)

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.860
I don't know. I don't
really think about that

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.120
to be real with you.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.630
I look at people's personalities more.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.850
I'd notice voices. That was a big thing.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.192
Or height is something
I can definitely notice.

00:05:32.192 --> 00:05:34.530
Obviously, if you end up
getting close to the person

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.099
and you start like feeling
on their face, by the way,

00:05:37.099 --> 00:05:39.212
blind people don't feel people's faces.

00:05:39.212 --> 00:05:40.620
That's not a thing.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.585
Unless you're super close to
someone that you're dating.

00:05:43.585 --> 00:05:48.570
You don't go around being
like, "Nice to meet you."

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:49.770
We don't do that.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.410
Nobody wants to feel somebody's oily skin.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:53.836
- Is your disability, would you say,

00:05:53.836 --> 00:05:56.100
accurately presented in the media?

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.210
- Definitely not.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:58.043
I don't know.

00:05:58.043 --> 00:05:59.443
People always think that blind person

00:05:59.443 --> 00:06:01.080
has a cane and sunglasses.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.256
A lot of the time, people
aren't using the canes correctly

00:06:05.256 --> 00:06:07.012
or rarely at all.

00:06:07.012 --> 00:06:10.217
- I'm actually someone who's
also considered disabled.

00:06:10.217 --> 00:06:12.120
- Really?
- Yeah, I was born

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.427
with a smaller left hand.

00:06:13.427 --> 00:06:14.659
- Oh, wow.
- So I thought this would be

00:06:14.659 --> 00:06:16.946
a great conversation to have.
- That's so cool.

00:06:16.946 --> 00:06:19.079
Disabled buddies.

00:06:19.079 --> 00:06:20.280
- (laughs) Yeah, exactly.
- Here for it.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:21.113
- Let's start a club.

00:06:21.113 --> 00:06:22.470
We could do it.
- Let's do it.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:25.260
- How have you felt about
the term disabled growing up?

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.030
- I identify as a blind person.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.970
And so, people will say handicapped,

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.920
which honestly feels way more limiting

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.900
and I don't like the use of that word.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.550
Disability is not a bad word.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.050
Disabled is not a bad word.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.240
And I think society's
been taught that it is.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.793
- Is there anything that
people without a disability

00:06:42.793 --> 00:06:47.793
could do to help make people
with a disability lives easier?

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.220
- Ask questions.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:53.430
There's this whole thing where
if a child asks a question

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:54.990
about somebody they see
that's different than them,

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:55.823
their parents are like,

00:06:55.823 --> 00:06:57.810
"Sh, don't ask that. Don't say that."

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.610
We want you to ask

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:00.933
because how are they gonna know otherwise?

00:07:00.933 --> 00:07:01.766
- Right.

00:07:01.766 --> 00:07:03.898
- I work with small children.

00:07:03.898 --> 00:07:06.661
A lot of the questions I got
when they saw me in person was,

00:07:06.661 --> 00:07:08.704
"Miss. Precious. What's
wrong with your eyes?

00:07:08.704 --> 00:07:11.250
What happened to your eyes? Do they hurt?"

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.410
- Yeah. I'm actually an
elementary school teacher.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.785
- Oh, no way. I'm elementary

00:07:15.785 --> 00:07:17.940
music school teacher.
- Wow.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.417
A kid walked up to me and he's like,

00:07:19.417 --> 00:07:20.559
"Your hand's smaller than mine."

00:07:20.559 --> 00:07:21.900
(both laughing)

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.880
I was like, "Wow. Yeah,
you're totally right."

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.500
I was like, "Let's high five right now."

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.960
They take it as like a sense of wonder

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.828
and a sense of coolness.
- The solution is

00:07:29.828 --> 00:07:32.280
adults need to be more like kids.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.290
- What do you consider your superpower?

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:38.883
- I think my superpower is love.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.269
I just try to spread love
with everything I do.

00:07:42.269 --> 00:07:44.039
- I can tell you have a lot of it.

00:07:44.039 --> 00:07:47.850
I wanna hug you through
even though I can't see you.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:50.691
- With platforms like TikTok and Instagram

00:07:50.691 --> 00:07:52.620
coming out with lots
of new content creators

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:53.490
with disabilities,

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.830
would you say that's helped change

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:57.840
people's perspectives on
people with disabilities?

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.670
- There's something
called inspiration porn.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.760
And what that means is
that people have this story

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:03.847
about a disability, like,

00:08:03.847 --> 00:08:06.788
"Oh, this poor person with a disability,

00:08:06.788 --> 00:08:10.920
they got taken to prom by
this fully functioning person.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.080
And that's such a heartwarming thing."

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.520
What if they were just two friends

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:15.829
that wanted to go to prom?

00:08:15.829 --> 00:08:18.300
Why is that newsworthy?

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.717
Tell me I'm inspirational for things

00:08:19.717 --> 00:08:22.518
that I do that I've
accomplished as a human.

00:08:22.518 --> 00:08:23.379
- Yeah, for sure.
- But don't tell me

00:08:23.379 --> 00:08:26.400
I'm inspirational for
getting up in the morning.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.720
And it's stressful
because the general public

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:31.734
is so unaware and ignorant

00:08:31.734 --> 00:08:33.900
when it comes to people with disabilities,

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.960
it is the one diversity that is left out

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.030
of every diversity conversation.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:44.030
It is the one thing that
people are still weird about.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.750
- What are some solutions
you think would help that

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.050
in order to better educate people?

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:51.900
- Equal opportunity for disabled people,

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:54.630
unemployment rate for blind people is 70%

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:59.400
because people think that we
can't do X, Y, Z in a job.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.840
And therefore, discriminate.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.438
- I had a question for you regarding

00:09:02.438 --> 00:09:06.228
how you you view or perceive
racism or discrimination

00:09:06.228 --> 00:09:08.340
based on the way that people look,

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:09.930
since you've never been able to see them.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:11.220
- Good question.

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:12.870
There's this misconception

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.357
that blind people can't be racist.

00:09:15.357 --> 00:09:17.850
That's not true because
there are blind people

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.110
that are racist. (laughs)

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.000
As a person of color myself,

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.320
being surrounded by so much
diversity my entire life

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.397
and representing my community
in the way that I do,

00:09:27.397 --> 00:09:29.520
I'm sensitive to these things

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.160
and I've experienced these things.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:34.785
I did experience bullying

00:09:34.785 --> 00:09:37.500
throughout elementary a little bit,

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:38.880
but mostly middle school.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.276
I had people just accuse me
of getting special treatment

00:09:42.276 --> 00:09:45.540
and take my tray out from
in front of me at lunch

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.130
because I couldn't see them doing it.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:50.269
And then I'd go to find my
food and nothing was there.

00:09:50.269 --> 00:09:51.340
- Oh my God.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:54.690
- So what advice do you
have for a disabled person

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:56.280
watching this right now who doesn't feel

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:58.301
like they have a lot of
help in certain areas?

00:09:58.301 --> 00:10:01.260
- I would say, start small.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.020
Whatever is attainable
right now toward the goal

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.851
that you wanna reach, figure
out what you can do right now

00:10:06.851 --> 00:10:08.610
and work towards it.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.110
You're not alone in this community,

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:11.533
we are on your side, we are with you.

00:10:11.533 --> 00:10:13.590
Keep going because you can do it.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.720
If we can do it, you can do it.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.299
- Absolutely. I agree.

00:10:17.299 --> 00:10:19.882
(upbeat music)

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.360
- [Android] If given the
opportunity to see one thing,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.520
what would you want to see?

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:32.357
- I would like to see happy babies

00:10:32.357 --> 00:10:34.830
because every time I'm nervous

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:36.420
when I'm performing or something,

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.306
I envision a bunch of
happy babies clapping

00:10:38.306 --> 00:10:40.648
and it makes me happy and I
wonder if they look as cute

00:10:40.648 --> 00:10:42.110
as I think they do.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:44.490
- [Android] Do you enjoy
going out to the movies?

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:45.870
What's your favorite movie?

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:47.250
- So, I do.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:50.520
